<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heraclius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 575–<a href="/years/641">641 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heraclius was a Byzantine emperor of the early seventh century who fought a long war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>. After major Persian advances, he campaigned deep into Persian territory and won a decisive victory in 627, forcing peace. The exhausting war left the Sasanian Empire weakened shortly before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Arab conquests</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heraclius.jpg" alt="Heraclius" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heraclius_solidus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Scholars debate the origin of Heraclius, chiefly because they debate that of his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius-the-elder">Heraclius the Elder</a>, who is often considered of Armenian descent. Little is known of his life before he became emperor. In 608 the elder Heraclius, Exarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, renounced his loyalty to the unpopular emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocas">Phocas</a>. His nephew Nicetas invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, while the younger Heraclius sailed east with a second force. When he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> the Excubitors deserted to him, and he entered the city without serious resistance. Phocas was executed, and Heraclius was crowned on 5 October 610.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The war with Sasanian Persia had begun in 602, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> used the murder of the emperor Maurice as an occasion to attack. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 613, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 614, where they captured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> by 622. Heraclius himself was defeated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> in 613. He raised money by borrowing Church treasure and cutting other spending, and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 4 April 622 to campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. In 626 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, supported by a Persian army, besieged Constantinople, and the siege failed.</p><p>Late in 627 Heraclius launched a winter offensive into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>. Khosrow was overthrown and killed by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavad-ii">Kavad II</a>, who sued for peace and agreed to withdraw from all occupied territory. In 629 Heraclius restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. The recovered lands were soon exposed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>: after the Battle of Yarmouk in 636 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> was lost within three years, and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> had fallen by 641. He also promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monothelitism">Monothelitism</a> in his Ecthesis, hoping to reconcile the Miaphysites, but the popes condemned it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Heraclius died of an illness on 11 February 641, aged sixty-six, and was succeeded by his sons Heraclius Constantine and Heraclonas. Edward Gibbon judged his reign inconsistent, with a brilliant middle between idle first and last years. Warren Treadgold praises his success against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and thinks his performance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> respectable, since he preserved the empire where the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-iii">Yazdegerd III</a> lost his. Greek became the official language of the state during his reign, and in 629 he began to use the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basileus">Basileus</a>. Western medieval Christians remembered him as the first crusader, for the recovery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heraclius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the last great war between Rome and Persia, the Sasanian Empire conquered Egypt, taking Alexandria in about 619, and held it as Sasanian Egypt until <b>Heraclius</b> negotiated its return in 629.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Heraclius</b>'s counter-offensive from 622 CE reversed these gains within six years, culminating in the Battle of Nineveh on 12 December 627 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Some scholars have suggested that the dome was added to an existing building, either one built by Muawiyah I or a Byzantine building from the time of <b>Heraclius</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>The Synaxarion ties the feast to the year 626, when Constantinople, in the reign of <b>Heraclius</b>, was attacked by the Persians and the Avars and was saved, it says, by the Theotokos; it also credits her with two later victories, under Leo III the Isaurian and Constantine Pogonatus.</blockquote><a href="/songs/akathist">Akathist Hymn</a></li><li><blockquote>The Byzantine emperor <b>Heraclius</b> routed a Sasanian army near the ruins of Nineveh in Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-12">12 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Kavad II overthrew his father, Khosrow II, the last great king of the Sasanian Empire, after the defeat by <b>Heraclius</b>, and had him killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-25">25 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cappadocia, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.67,34.84&amp;year=617" aria-label="Cappadocia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5415 776 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Heraclius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5865 1046h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5865 1046h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.67,34.84&amp;year=617">What was here in 617 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
